  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does a green railway signal mean?",
      "a": "Green means the line ahead is clear for at least the next two blocks, so the driver can run at the normal speed for that line.",
      "asks": [
        "Can a train go at full speed on green?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a yellow railway signal mean?",
      "a": "A single yellow means go on but be ready to stop at the next signal, which is showing red. On busy lines a double yellow gives an earlier warning.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a double yellow mean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a red railway signal mean?",
      "a": "Red means stop: the block ahead may have a train in it. Passing a red signal is a serious event, and most lines have systems that automatically brake a train that does.",
      "asks": [
        "What happens if a train passes a red signal?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Who controls railway signals?",
      "a": "Many signals change automatically as trains pass. Signallers in control centres set routes at junctions, and computer interlocking stops them setting routes that clash.",
      "asks": [
        "Are railway signals automatic?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does the railway know where a train is?",
      "a": "Track circuits pass a small current through the rails; a train's wheels and axles short it out, showing the block is occupied. Axle counters instead count wheels into and out of each block.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a track circuit?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Railways divide lines into blocks so that only one train is in each block at a time.",
    "Many UK main lines use four-aspect signals: red, single yellow, double yellow and green.",
    "Track circuits detect trains because the wheels and axles connect the two rails electrically."
  ],
